{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc | sed -n '487,610p;705,780p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc | sed -n '408,545p'; sed -n '660,720p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md; sed -n '265,380p;530,625p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; rg -n 'defn.*(body|rd|uses|tail-call|parse)' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/code.cljc; nl -ba test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc | sed -n '92,108p;251,304p'\"","aggregated_output":"   487\t   is the concatenation's H at the time, informative only. Free\n   488\t   environment, store, primitives, and modules are not registers and stay\n   489\t   on the state.\"\n   490\t  [vm pc' stack']\n   491\t  (cond-> {:segment (:segment vm),\n   492\t           :pc pc',\n   493\t           :frames (:frames vm),\n   494\t           :stack stack',\n   495\t           :continuation (:continuation vm),\n   496\t           :format format-tag,\n   497\t           :hash (:hash vm),\n   498\t           :image (nth (row-at (:images vm) pc') 0 nil)}\n   499\t    (:store-of vm) (assoc :store-of (:store-of vm))))\n   500\t\n   501\t\n   502\t(defn- refuse-continuation!\n   503\t  [base entry]\n   504\t  (throw (ex-info \"Cannot restore a continuation of another model or image\"\n   505\t                  {:reason :foreign-format,\n   506\t                   :rule :continuation-format,\n   507\t                   :format (:format entry),\n   508\t                   :hash (:hash entry),\n   509\t                   :image (:image entry),\n   510\t                   :expected-format format-tag,\n   511\t                   :expected-hash (:hash base),\n   512\t                   :images (:images base)})))\n   513\t\n   514\t\n   515\t(defn stack-restore\n   516\t  \"This machine's one restore function, `base entry val -> state`\n   517\t   (`yin.vm.engine.md` section 2.1; design section 4.1, item 4). `base` is\n   518\t   the state after the engine has done its part; `entry` is the wait, ready,\n   519\t   or parked record; `val` is the value the parked instruction receives.\n   520\t\n   521\t   It first refuses with a qualified `:continuation-format` outcome unless\n   522\t   the entry's `:format` is `:yin.debruijn.code` and its `:image` is a row\n   523\t   of the offset table (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r6): a continuation\n   524\t   from the named VM, from the register kernel, or from an image this\n   525\t   kernel does not hold is never interpreted here. `:hash` is never a\n   526\t   restore key: it changes at every `attach-image`, while the table only\n   527\t   grows, so an entry parked before an attach still names its row.\n   528\t\n   529\t   Then the FFI two-step, on the entry keys the `yin.vm.ffi` convention\n   530\t   owns, in the semantic VM's placement:\n   531\t\n   532\t   - A `:request-sent` entry is a writer whose retained request has now been\n   533\t     appended. It must not resume: its registers are re-parked as the\n   534\t     call-out reader (`ffi/response-wait-entry`) and the machine stays\n   535\t     blocked.\n   536\t   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\n   537\t     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it and checks correlation, and\n   538\t     the parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\n   539\t\n   540\t   Otherwise the registers are written from the entry and `val` is conjed\n   541\t   onto the restored `:stack` -- the B3 obligation that every\n   542\t   value-producing opcode lands on the stack, since there is no\n   543\t   accumulator. `:segment` is never written from the entry: the code space\n   544\t   is kernel state, and since appending moves nothing, the entry's\n   545\t   absolute pcs and stack bases still name what they named.\"\n   546\t  [base entry val]\n   547\t  (when-not (and (= format-tag (:format entry))\n   548\t                 (some #(= (:image entry) (nth % 0)) (:images base)))\n   549\t    (refuse-continuation! base entry))\n   550\t  (if (:request-sent entry)\n   551\t    (-> base\n   552\t        (update :wait-set (fnil conj [])\n   553\t                (ffi/response-wait-entry entry (:call-id entry)))\n   554\t        (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n   555\t               :blocked? true\n   556\t               :halted? false))\n   557\t    (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n   558\t          base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n   559\t          val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)]\n   560\t      (assoc base\n   561\t             :pc (:pc entry)\n   562\t             :frames (:frames entry)\n   563\t             :continuation (:continuation entry)\n   564\t             :stack (conj (vec (:stack entry)) val)\n   565\t             :store-of (:store-of entry)\n   566\t             :value val\n   567\t             ;; Restored registers are active work: a driver resuming a\n   568\t             ;; halted machine directly through `engine/resume-continuation`\n   569\t             ;; gets a running one back, as the semantic VM's restore gives.\n   570\t             :halted? false))))\n   571\t\n   572\t\n   573\t(defn- park-entry-fns\n   574\t  \"Wait-entry builders for an instruction whose effect may block (design\n   575\t   section 4.1, item 5): each closes over the post-instruction registers\n   576\t   and returns them merged with `:reason` and the resource ids from the\n   577\t   handler's result. Nothing else is attached: no handle, no closure, no\n   578\t   restore function.\"\n   579\t  [vm pc' stack']\n   580\t  (let [regs (registers vm pc' stack')]\n   581\t    {:stream/put (fn [_state _effect result]\n   582\t                   (assoc regs\n   583\t                          :reason :put\n   584\t                          :stream-id (:stream-id result))),\n   585\t     :stream/next (fn [_state _effect result]\n   586\t                    (assoc regs\n   587\t                           :reason :next\n   588\t                           :cursor-ref (:cursor-ref result)\n   589\t                           :stream-id (:stream-id result))),\n   590\t     :module/require (fn [_state _effect _result] regs)}))\n   591\t\n   592\t\n   593\t(defn- run-effect\n   594\t  \"Dispatch one effect through the engine from the instruction at `(:pc vm)`\n   595\t   with `stack'` its operand stack after popping the instruction's operands.\n   596\t   On a value the machine continues at pc+1 with the value on the stack; on\n   597\t   a park the engine has already placed the pure-data entry in the wait set\n   598\t   and marked the machine blocked, and the registers mirror that entry.\"\n   599\t  [vm effect stack']\n   600\t  (let [pc' (inc (:pc vm))\n   601\t        {:keys [state value blocked?]}\n   602\t        (engine/handle-effect vm\n   603\t                              effect\n   604\t                              {:park-entry-fns (park-entry-fns vm pc' stack')})]\n   605\t    (if blocked?\n   606\t      (assoc state :pc pc' :stack stack')\n   607\t      (assoc state :pc pc' :stack (conj stack' value)))))\n   608\t\n   609\t\n   610\t(defn- ffi-call\n   705\t      ;; split: every value-producing case already conjes its result\n   706\t      ;; straight onto `:stack`. By the time control reaches a `:push`,\n   707\t      ;; the value it would push is already there -- so here it is a no-op\n   708\t      ;; that only advances `pc`. Every value-producing opcode below\n   709\t      ;; (:stream-*, :gensym, :current-continuation, :resume, and the\n   710\t      ;; restore after :ffi-call) honours this, or a `:push` immediately\n   711\t      ;; after it would silently drop the value -- there is no `val`\n   712\t      ;; register to recover it from.\n   713\t      :push\n   714\t      (assoc vm :pc (inc pc))\n   715\t\n   716\t      :call\n   717\t      (let [argc (nth inst 1), tail? (nth inst 2)\n   718\t            total (count stack)\n   719\t            f-pos (- total argc 1)\n   720\t            f (nth stack f-pos)\n   721\t            args (subvec stack (inc f-pos) total)\n   722\t            stack' (subvec stack 0 f-pos)]\n   723\t        (case (engine/operator-kind vm f)\n   724\t          :closure\n   725\t          (let [c (values/payload f)\n   726\t                locals (bind-positional (:arity c) args)\n   727\t                body-frames (conj (:frames c) locals)\n   728\t                continuation' (if tail?\n   729\t                                continuation\n   730\t                                (conj continuation\n   731\t                                      (cond-> {:return-pc (inc pc),\n   732\t                                               :frames frames,\n   733\t                                               :stack-base (count stack')}\n   734\t                                        store-of (assoc :store-of store-of))))]\n   735\t            (assoc vm\n   736\t                   :pc (:body-pc c)\n   737\t                   :frames body-frames\n   738\t                   :stack stack'\n   739\t                   :continuation continuation'\n   740\t                   :store-of (:store-of c)))\n   741\t\n   742\t          ;; A primitive host function, resolved by :load-free. The same\n   743\t          ;; path the named engine's `apply-call` uses for a `fn?` callee:\n   744\t          ;; a plain result lands on the stack; an effect descriptor (a\n   745\t          ;; `require`, a `stream` module call) is dispatched\n   746\t          ;; through the engine and may park with the continuation after\n   747\t          ;; the call site.\n   748\t          :host-fn\n   749\t          (let [result (apply f args)]\n   750\t            (if (module/effect? result)\n   751\t              (run-effect (engine/check-callee-effect! vm f result)\n   752\t                          result\n   753\t                          stack')\n   754\t              (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack' result))))\n   755\t\n   756\t          ;; A captured continuation: abortive, so the call site's stack,\n   757\t          ;; frames and return frames are dropped and the captured\n   758\t          ;; registers are restored with the argument on the stack, as a\n   759\t          ;; resume would deliver it. The store is not a register.\n   760\t          :continuation\n   761\t          (stack-restore vm\n   762\t                         (values/payload f)\n   763\t                         (engine/continuation-argument args))))\n   764\t\n   765\t      :return\n   766\t      (let [val (peek stack)\n   767\t            frame (peek continuation)]\n   768\t        (if frame\n   769\t          (assoc vm\n   770\t                 :pc (:return-pc frame)\n   771\t                 :frames (:frames frame)\n   772\t                 :stack (conj (subvec stack 0 (:stack-base frame)) val)\n   773\t                 :continuation (pop continuation)\n   774\t                 :store-of (:store-of frame))\n   775\t          (assoc vm\n   776\t                 :halted? true, :stack [val], :value val, :store-of nil)))\n   777\t\n   778\t      :jump\n   779\t      (assoc vm :pc (nth inst 1))\n   780\t\n   408\t   in flight stays attached, and the absolute `:return-pc` still names\n   409\t   the caller's instruction in the grown code space.\"\n   410\t  [vm val]\n   411\t  (let [continuation (:continuation vm)]\n   412\t    (if (seq continuation)\n   413\t      (let [frame (peek continuation)\n   414\t            return-pc (:return-pc frame)\n   415\t            body (body-of-pc (:segment vm) return-pc)\n   416\t            reg-count (:registers body)\n   417\t            dest (:dest frame)\n   418\t            restored-regs (reduce (fn [acc [r v]] (assoc acc r v))\n   419\t                                  (vec (repeat reg-count nil))\n   420\t                                  (:regs frame))\n   421\t            final-regs (if (some? dest)\n   422\t                         (assoc restored-regs dest val)\n   423\t                         restored-regs)]\n   424\t        (assoc vm\n   425\t               :pc return-pc\n   426\t               :frames (:frames frame)\n   427\t               :registers final-regs\n   428\t               :continuation (pop continuation)\n   429\t               :store-of (:store-of frame)\n   430\t               :value val\n   431\t               :halted? false))\n   432\t      (assoc vm :halted? true, :value val, :store-of nil))))\n   433\t\n   434\t\n   435\t(defn- refuse-continuation!\n   436\t  [base entry]\n   437\t  (throw (ex-info \"Cannot restore a continuation of another model or image\"\n   438\t                  {:reason :foreign-format,\n   439\t                   :rule :continuation-format,\n   440\t                   :format (:format entry),\n   441\t                   :hash (:hash entry),\n   442\t                   :image (:image entry),\n   443\t                   :expected-format format-tag,\n   444\t                   :expected-hash (:hash base),\n   445\t                   :images (:images base)})))\n   446\t\n   447\t\n   448\t(defn- check-format!\n   449\t  \"Refuse `entry` unless it is this model's payload for a row of the offset\n   450\t   table (r6).\"\n   451\t  [base entry]\n   452\t  (when-not (and (= format-tag (:format entry))\n   453\t                 (some #(= (:image entry) (nth % 0)) (:images base)))\n   454\t    (refuse-continuation! base entry)))\n   455\t\n   456\t\n   457\t(defn- write-back\n   458\t  \"Write `entry`'s registers back and deliver `val` via `:write-result` or\n   459\t   `:return-result`. Registers only: `:segment` is kernel state.\"\n   460\t  [base entry val]\n   461\t  (if (= :return-result (:resume-mode entry))\n   462\t    (return-transition (assoc base\n   463\t                              :pc (:pc entry)\n   464\t                              :frames (:frames entry)\n   465\t                              :continuation (:continuation entry)\n   466\t                              :store-of (:store-of entry)\n   467\t                              :halted? false)\n   468\t                       val)\n   469\t    (let [pc (:pc entry)\n   470\t          body (body-of-pc (:segment base) pc)\n   471\t          reg-count (:registers body)\n   472\t          dest (:dest entry)\n   473\t          restored-regs\n   474\t          (reduce (fn [acc [r v]] (assoc acc r v))\n   475\t                  (vec (repeat reg-count nil))\n   476\t                  (:regs entry))\n   477\t          final-regs (if (some? dest)\n   478\t                       (assoc restored-regs dest val)\n   479\t                       restored-regs)]\n   480\t      (assoc base\n   481\t             :pc pc\n   482\t             :frames (:frames entry)\n   483\t             :registers final-regs\n   484\t             :continuation (:continuation entry)\n   485\t             :store-of (:store-of entry)\n   486\t             :value val\n   487\t             :halted? false))))\n   488\t\n   489\t\n   490\t(defn register-restore\n   491\t  \"The register VM's restore function, `base entry val -> state`\n   492\t   (design section 5.2.3). Validates format identity and that the entry's\n   493\t   `:image` is a row of the offset table (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3,\n   494\t   r6; `:hash` is never a restore key, since it changes at every\n   495\t   `attach-image`), checks that the payload is defect-free via\n   496\t   effects/continuation-defect, checks that `val` is plain data, handles\n   497\t   the FFI two-step, and delivers `val` via `:write-result` or\n   498\t   `:return-result`. The entry restores registers only and never assigns\n   499\t   `:segment`: the code space is kernel state.\"\n   500\t  [base entry val]\n   501\t  (check-format! base entry)\n   502\t  (when-let [defect (effects/continuation-defect entry)]\n   503\t    (throw (ex-info \"Corrupt or tampered continuation payload\"\n   504\t                    defect)))\n   505\t  (when-not (vm/machine-data? val)\n   506\t    (throw (ex-info \"Resume value must be plain data\"\n   507\t                    {:rule :resume-value, :val val})))\n   508\t  (if (:request-sent entry)\n   509\t    (-> base\n   510\t        (update :wait-set (fnil conj [])\n   511\t                (ffi/response-wait-entry entry (:call-id entry)))\n   512\t        (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n   513\t               :blocked? true\n   514\t               :halted? false))\n   515\t    (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n   516\t          base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n   517\t          val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)]\n   518\t      (write-back base entry val))))\n   519\t\n   520\t\n   521\t;; =============================================================================\n   522\t;; Effects & FFI dispatch\n   523\t;; =============================================================================\n   524\t\n   525\t(defn- payload-of\n   526\t  \"The continuation payload of `inst` (`effects/continuation-payload`),\n   527\t   carrying the running body's module store when there is one.\"\n   528\t  [vm inst]\n   529\t  (cond-> (effects/continuation-payload vm inst)\n   530\t    (:store-of vm) (assoc :store-of (:store-of vm))))\n   531\t\n   532\t\n   533\t(defn- park-entry-fns\n   534\t  [payload]\n   535\t  {:stream/put (fn [_state effect result]\n   536\t                 (assoc payload\n   537\t                        :reason :put\n   538\t                        :stream-id (:stream-id result)\n   539\t                        :datom (:val effect))),\n   540\t   :stream/next (fn [_state _effect result]\n   541\t                  (assoc payload\n   542\t                         :reason :next\n   543\t                         :cursor-ref (:cursor-ref result)\n   544\t                         :stream-id (:stream-id result))),\n   545\t   :module/require (fn [_state _effect _result] payload)})\n                    :yin.k/env {…} :yin.k/window {…} :yin.k/rd r} … ]\n :yin.k/pending  {…}}                    ; unchanged, §7.4.3\n\n;; a tail state (through-return delivery)\n{:yin.k/state    :tail\n :yin.k/segment  A :yin.k/site n         ; the tail call site, for provenance only\n :yin.k/reason   …\n :yin.k/deliver  {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n :yin.k/k        [ … ]                   ; the frames delivery will pop\n :yin.k/pending  {…}}\n```\n\n`:yin.k/val`, `:yin.k/stack` and `:stack-base` are gone. The window's\n**membership is validated**, not trusted: the receiver recomputes\n`saved(pc, rd)` from A (§8.1) and refuses a window with a register\noutside it (`:window-extra`), missing from it (`:window-missing`), or\ncontaining the delivery destination (`:window-self`). Those three are\nthe membership diagnostics; the full frame validator also checks\nrepresentation (integer ids, sorted unique keys), body scope (every\nregister of the window belongs to the body `pc` is in), site/pc pairing\n(`pc` is a safepoint successor of the right kind for `reason`;\n`site` is a tail call), destination equality (`rd` is the `rd` of the\ninstruction at `pc−1`), delivery mode (`:act` carries `:rd`, `:tail`\ncarries `:return` and no window or env), return-frame sites (each\n`:return` frame's `pc` follows a non-tail call whose `rd` it names, and\nits window is `saved(pc, rd)` of that body), `K` structure (frames\ninnermost last, each well-formed), and pending compatibility with\n`reason`. Two captures of equal state therefore encode identically, and\ndead values never travel.\n\nThe row carrier (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.2) changes\naccordingly: `:regs` becomes `[segment-A pc env-cref window-cref\nk-cref deliver-D]` for an `act` and a sibling `:tail-state [segment-A\nsite k-cref deliver-D]` is added; `deliver` is structural data of the\nclosed shape above; `:window` is a keyed row `[r₁ V₁ r₂ V₂ …]` sorted by\n`r`; `:kframe` becomes `[segment-A pc env-cref window-cref rd]`; and\n`:stack` and `stack-base` are removed. That amendment is written when\nthis design lands (§10 phase 6).\n\n### 4.3 Foreign engines (UCF §7.4.2)\n\nA foreign engine's static safepoint map keeps `:yin.safepoint/segment`,\n`:yin.safepoint/pc` and `:yin.safepoint/engine`, replaces\n`:stack-effect` with the per-pc **def and use sets** and `L(pc)` derived\nfrom A (§8.1), and keeps `:yin.safepoint/layout` as a map from physical\nlocation to virtual register. The reconstruction obligation is: for an\n`act` state, produce the segment `A` and resume `pc`, every register in\n`saved(pc, rd) = L(pc) − {rd}` (never the not-yet-produced\ndestination), `E`, `K` with each frame's validated window and `rd`, the\ndelivery record, the pending state, and the captured store context; for\na `tail` state, produce the segment and the tail `site`, `K` with its\nvalidated frames, the delivery record and the pending state, and **no**\ncurrent window or `E`. A physical-slot map alone does not discharge it\nwhen values are spilled, rematerialized, or shared between locations;\nthe engine owes the values, however it kept them. `lift(lower(frame)) =\nframe` holds for admitted canonical frames, as today.\n\n## 5. Scheduler, park, resume, FFI, modules\n\nUnchanged in meaning (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.4, §3.5; UCF §7.6.3):\nthe wait set, `:parked`, the fresh-name counter, the FFI pair, the\ncode address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cell id,\n`S` a symbol, `D` structural data (§4). Each `[:row B]` position admits\nonly the row kinds its table entry names; a `[:row B]` whose target is\nof another kind refuses (§8). Keyed rows (`:bindings`, `:map`, `:store`,\n`:parked-table`, `:cells`, the installs and module-stores slots) must\nhave unique keys, and `:set` rows unique elements, under canonical bytes\n**and** under portable decoded equality, since Jing's own map and set\nrules do not apply to a key or element list spelled as a vector.\n\n### 5.1 Values (content rows)\n\n| Kind | Slots | Decodes to |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:vec` | `V …` in order | vector |\n| `:list` | `V …` in order | list |\n| `:set` | `V …` sorted (§5 preamble) | set |\n| `:map` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …`, each `kᵢ` a `V`, sorted by key | map |\n| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁ S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | `{S V}` |\n| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | E: the named environment plus, when `store-of` is an `M`, `engine/store-of-key` (amendment §2) |\n| `:closure` | `segment-A  entry-pc-N  params  bindings-cref  store-of` | semantic closure (amendment §6 semantic arm); `params` is a plain vector of symbols checked against the addressed lambda |\n| `:prim` | `name-S` | the named primitive after the profile check (UCF §7.5.2); the profile lives in requires |\n| `:stream` | `identity  descriptor` | a stream marker; both plain, authenticated at lift and re-sealed at lower |\n| `:reified` | `regs-cref` | `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/registers R}`: a captured continuation, no wait |\n| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/parked-id P}`: resolves in the task's parked table; never duplicates `R` |\n\n`:reified` and `:parked-ref` keep the two outer discriminants amendment\n§6 requires and lower differently: a reified value becomes a\nreceiver-owned native continuation from `R`; a parked reference\nreconstructs the engine's parked value for `P`. A reified value's\nregisters are validated in captured-value context (amendment §7): its pc\nmust be a `:current-continuation` site's successor, not a wait or park\nsafepoint.\n\n### 5.2 Semantic registers, waits, frames (content rows)\n\n| Kind | Slots |\n|---|---|\n| `:regs` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-cref  k-cref` |\n| `:stack` | `V …` bottom first |\n| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` bottom to top |\n| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-base-N` |\n| `:wait` | `regs-cref  pending-cref` |\n\n`:regs` is amendment §5.1's `R` for the semantic profile, and nothing\nelse: no reason, no pending. A wait is a `:wait` row pairing registers\nwith a pending; a reified value and a parked record reference `:regs`\ndirectly. `:kframe` is exactly the semantic return frame `{:type :return\nsegment pc env stack-base}`; the semantic profile has no other frame\ntype on the wire (walker effect frames belong to the walker profile's\nown table). Resume pc and return pc must lie inside their segment, the\nreturn pc immediately after a non-tail call, stack bases nondecreasing\nfrom outermost to innermost and each at most the enclosing stack length\n(amendment §5.1), all checked in §8.\n\n### 5.3 Pending (content rows)\n\nOne kind, `:pending`, whose first slot is the reason and whose remaining\nslots are fixed per reason. The reasons are UCF §7.4.3's closed union as\nthe amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n`:call-effect` are not reasons and refuse as `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n| reason | slots after the reason |\n|---|---|\n| `:next` | `cell-C` |\n| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id-D` |\n| `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  envelope-V  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n| `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n| `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n| `:install` | `name-S` |\n\nThe retained FFI request's `envelope-V` is the complete envelope map,\nrecursively encoded under §4's ordinary rules: `[:lit m]` when it\nreaches no reference (the common case, arguments such as `[1 2]`), a\n`:map` row otherwise. Lower decodes it and retries it verbatim; it\nnever recomputes the arguments. Correlation must agree, not merely be present:\na cell's `:cell-body` stream is the pending's response stream; an\nenvelope's link id or call id equals the pending's; a `:link-request`\nenvelope names the pending's module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\npresent exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\nwriter, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\nand subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, unique across the task and its children, absent\nwhen the body has no origin). `request-envelope` and `envelope` are the\nretained requests, retried verbatim. `call-id` and `link-id` are plain.\nAn `:install` pending is never sufficient alone: the root's installs\nslot must hold the entry for `name`, else lift refuses\n`:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:incomplete-install` and a body carrying it\nis `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n### 5.4 Task context (content rows)\n\n| Kind | Slots |\n|---|---|\n| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by key; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n| `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n| `:parked-table` | `P₁ regs-cref₁ P₂ regs-cref₂ …` sorted by P |\n| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n| `:cells` | the census, §3 |\n| `:cell-body` | `stream-cref  position` (position in the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` portable cursor domain) |\n\nResource entries never appear in `:store`; streams and cells lower into\nthe receiver's private resource table beside the program store (linker\nM4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). `:module-store` rows are the per-module\nsnapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table holds exactly the\nreachable records. An `:install` row carries the complete entry of\n§7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, parent link id (nil where the\ncontract permits), the response verbatim as structural data, and the\nchild as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role. \"Verified\"\nmeans verified by the **receiver**, never trusted because it decodes:\nthe response's manifest names the module the row is keyed by, its link\nid equals `parent`, its image is well formed, hashes to the address it\nis delivered under, is the image the manifest names, and its format,\ncontract and the child's profile agree (`yin.vm.linker.md` §5, §9;\namendment §2). A failure is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row.\n\n     the root's installs and module-stores slots, keyed by module name\n     and `M`): `[kind :key CE(k)]` when descending into the key,\n     `[kind :val CE(k)]` when descending into the value; never an\n     index. For `:bindings`, `:parked-table`, installs and module\n     stores the key is a structural datum and `CE(k)` is the datum.\n   - *Task boundary*: a path never crosses into an install child; a\n     child's cells have paths rooted at the child's own root.\n   Equal occurrences yield equal positions, one per occurrence, and\n   positions never contain a source enumeration index of an unordered\n   container.\n4. **Signature and refinement.** A cell's signature is the CBOR of\n   `[:sig <old label> <sorted vector of its positions, duplicates\n   retained>]`; its new label is that byte string. Repeat until no\n   class splits. Keeping the old label makes refinement monotone, so\n   the partition stabilizes in at most `n − k` splitting rounds from\n   `k` initial classes plus one stability check; this bounds partition\n   rounds only, not signature-processing or search work.\n5. **Search.** Order classes bytewise by label. If every class is a\n   singleton, assign `1 … n` in that order and stop. Otherwise take the\n   first non-singleton class and, **for each of its members in turn**,\n   give that member the label that is the CBOR byte string of\n   `[:ind <depth> <old label>]`, where `depth` is 0 at the top-level\n   search and increases by one per level of recursion (distinct from\n   every refinement label by its tag; no ordering relative to other\n   labels is needed), refine to stability, and\n   recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete leaf yields a\n   numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the **root body's\n   canonical CBOR bytes** (the digest preimage, not the digest) as the\n   leaf's key. The procedure's output is the row set of the leaf with\n   the bytewise least key. Two leaves with equal keys have emitted\n   identical rows: they differ only in which source cell received\n   which number, which the transported representation cannot observe.\n\n**Validation.** Canonicality is a property of the emitted rows, not of\na source-cell-to-number bijection, so the receiver does not compare\nnumberings. It decodes the rows to the abstract task, runs the\nprocedure, re-emits rows, and compares the resulting root id with the\ncarried root id: unequal is `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n`:noncanonical-cells`. Every labeling that yields the selected\ncommitment is thereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever\ndecides validity. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\nkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with a structured resource refusal before\nany attachment and never labels the state `:noncanonical-cells`, never\nfalls back to encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check.\n\nUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\ndifferent states sharing a closure over the same cell may number it\ndifferently and so not share that closure's row across lifts; that\ncross-lift dedup is knowingly given up for identity-bearing values and\nnever affected rows below a cell. On lower, each `C` becomes one fresh\nprivate resource entry seeded at its carried position; every `[:cell C]`\nmaps to that entry; two references to one cell share it and one\nreference per cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n\n## 7. Equality and the content address\n\nThe transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\nthe root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cell\nid through the references, and to every cell's contents through the\ncensus. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,\nand under §6 that holds iff they were lifted from states equal up to the\nrenaming of cell ids that §6 makes canonical. No physical enumeration of\nthe row set needs an order, because the root commits to everything and\nthe pack, if one is addressed, has its own canonical encoding (§10.2).\nJing supplies canonical bytes and the digest of each body; this document\nsupplies the order within each body and the numbering of cells. Jing is\nasked to sort nothing it does not already sort.\n\nOccurrence and origin are inside the hash, as in versions 1 and 2 where\nthe body address is the hash of the whole body. Snapshot variants of one\noccurrence may therefore have distinct addresses while remaining one\noccurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n\n## 8. Validation on lower\n\nValidation is pure until the last step and checks the whole tree before\none attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n\n1. **Gate.** Decode the wire map's canonical bytes retaining numeric\n   classes; the tag is present and the outer version is the exact\n   integer 3, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (version found, supported\n   set). Reject duplicate row ids and a `:yin.k/root` that names no\n   `:task` row (`:yin.k/undecodable`). Walk install children from the\n   root in canonical module-name order with an **active recursion-path\n   set** (§3), following only `:install` child crefs; a cref outside the\n   rows, a child already on the active path, or a non-`:task` child\n   stops the gate as `:yin.k/undecodable`; a child reached again off\n   the path is gated once and its result cached. Gate every\n   child's version, then every profile map against the registry (only\n   the semantic profile is supported, §2), then require all versions 3\n   and all profiles equal to the root's. Malformed task structure is\n   `:yin.k/undecodable`; an unsupported version or profile is\n   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; the two are never conflated.\n2. **Structure, non-recursive.** Every row's kind is tabled with the\n   right arity; every structural slot holds its tabled kind by local\n73:(defn rd\n81:(defn uses\n97:(defn tail-call?\n256:(defn body-ranges\n279:(defn body-index\n350:(defn- parse-range\n409:(defn- parse-body\n421:(defn- parse-bodies\n438:(defn- body-partition\n506:(defn- body-pcs\n856:(defn parse\n    92\t(def ^:private corpus\n    93\t  \"Each segment binds `cat` around its whole body, so a row's parking\n    94\t   expression may combine through it too.\"\n    95\t  (mapv\n    96\t    (fn [[label seg]] [label (comp with-cat seg)])\n    97\t    [[:bare identity]\n    98\t     [:operand (fn [op] (cat-of (s/lit [:operand]) op))]\n    99\t     [:depth-3 (fn [op] (nest 3 op))]\n   100\t     [:captured\n   101\t      (fn [op]\n   102\t        (s/let1 'k (s/lit :captured)\n   103\t                (s/let1 'f (s/lam ['y] (cat-of (cat-of (cat-of (s/lit []) (s/v 'k))\n   104\t                                                       (s/v 'y))\n   105\t                                               op))\n   106\t                        (s/app (s/v 'f) (s/lit :arg)))))]]))\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   251\t(defn- stream-call-row\n   252\t  \"An effectful call of the stream module's `next!` -- ordinary, or\n   253\t   `tail?` in the tail position of a closure -- parked on its read.\"\n   254\t  [tail?]\n   255\t  (fn [engine seg]\n   256\t    (let [call (cond-> (s/app (s/v 'stream/next!) (s/v 'c)) tail? (assoc :tail? true))\n   257\t          op (if tail?\n   258\t               (s/app (s/lam ['u] call) (s/lit 0))\n   259\t               (cat-of (s/lit [:ordinary]) call))\n   260\t          m (vm/run (s/load-ast engine (s/new-machine engine {:modules stream-modules})\n   261\t                                (s/let1 's {:type :stream/make, :buffer 4}\n   262\t                                        (s/let1 'c (s/cursor-of (s/v 's))\n   263\t                                                (seg op)))))\n   264\t          src (s/stream-of m)]\n   265\t      {:machine m\n   266\t       :kind :blocked\n   267\t       :reason :next\n   268\t       :steps [#(stream/append! src \"B\")]\n   269\t       :receiver (constantly {:opts {:modules stream-modules}})\n   270\t       :trace (atom [])})))\n   271\t\n   272\t\n   273\t(defn- park-row\n   274\t  \"An explicit park: resumed by its record's id, not by a wait.\"\n   275\t  [engine seg]\n   276\t  (let [m (vm/run (s/load-ast engine (s/new-machine engine) (seg {:type :vm/park})))\n   277\t        pid (:id (vm/value m))]\n   278\t    {:machine m\n   279\t     :kind :parked\n   280\t     :resume (fn [m']\n   281\t               (s/run-beside engine m' {:type :vm/resume, :parked-id pid,\n   282\t                                        :val (s/lit \"back\")}))\n   283\t     :trace (atom [])}))\n   284\t\n   285\t\n   286\t(defn- halt-row\n   287\t  [engine seg]\n   288\t  {:machine (vm/run (s/load-ast engine (s/new-machine engine) (seg (s/lit 42))))\n   289\t   :kind :halted\n   290\t   :trace (atom [])})\n   291\t\n   292\t\n   293\t(def ^:private rows\n   294\t  \"The liftable rows of 7.4.1, by name: every row runs on every lane.\"\n   295\t  [[:explicit-park park-row]\n   296\t   [:blocked-read read-row]\n   297\t   [:blocked-write write-row]\n   298\t   [:sent-ffi sent-ffi-row]\n   299\t   [:retained-ffi retained-ffi-row]\n   300\t   [:ordinary-require (require-row false)]\n   301\t   [:tail-require (require-row true)]\n   302\t   [:ordinary-stream-call (stream-call-row false)]\n   303\t   [:tail-stream-call (stream-call-row true)]\n   304\t   [:halt halt-row]])\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
