m-phase4a': semantic-register-vm slice 4a, the core register evaluator\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-bdgnSsqZ' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-10-11 01:44:33.279 xcodebuild[23825:55418618]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-10-11 01:44:33.518 xcodebuild[23825:55418617] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-ALF9N77f' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-10-11 01:44:34.115 xcodebuild[23828:55418632]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-10-11 01:44:34.318 xcodebuild[23828:55418631] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n261fce5a docs(yin.vm): phase-6 design, the continuation format and the row carrier\n   750\toracle for the first law.\n   751\t\n   752\t## 10. Questions the review answered, and what remains\n   753\t\n   754\tAnswers 1–6 are the codex Architect's (round 1); where the Architect\n   755\tmarked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n   756\t\n   757\t1. **Occurrence inside the hash.** Kept inside, following the governing\n   758\t   rule and binding custody context to the published checkpoint. An\n   759\t   occurrence-independent execution-state address is an owner-level\n   760\t   protocol decision; if ever pursued it must sit beside, not replace, a\n   761\t   fully authenticated checkpoint address, and it does not substitute\n   762\t   for the census.\n   763\t2. **Packing.** Individual immutable content rows plus a self-contained\n   764\t   transport pack. When a pack is addressed it gets its own address and\n   765\t   canonical encoding, distinct from the semantic root id; cell ids\n   766\t   remain root-scoped inside it. Same answer as code-as-tuples §2.1\n   767\t   should receive; the loader interface is resolved explicitly, not by\n   768\t   layout.\n   769\t3. **The nested grammar.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 remain compatibility\n   770\t   inputs. One canonical nested projection of version 3 is kept for\n   771\t   single-message channels and must stay exactly mutually inverse with\n   772\t   the rows. Rows are the carrier authority once specified. Retiring old\n   773\t   readers is an owner version-support decision.\n   774\t4. **Contract stamp.** The profile map and code contracts do not move; a\n   775\t   carrier change is a new body version with its own gate. Any change to\n   776\t   instruction or scheduler semantics would separately revise the\n   777\t   execution contract and is not proposed here.\n   778\t5. **Reified and parked.** Sharing immutable `:regs` rows is acceptable;\n   779\t   the two keep distinct outer kinds (§5.1) and distinct lowering. A\n   780\t   reified capture has no pending; a parked reference resolves in task\n   781\t   context.\n   782\t6. **Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before any implementation\n   783\t   brief claims heterogeneous execution conformance, and not drafted\n   784\t   here: admitted values, numeric behaviour, key equality, metadata,\n   785\t   resource and profile requirements, refusal sets, and safepoint\n   786\t   reconstruction obligations. Slot losslessness alone does not\n   787\t   establish execution parity, and version 3, like version 2, does not\n   788\t   translate between kernels.\n   789\t\n   790\tAnswers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted:\n   791\t\n   792\t7. **Other profile tables.** Deferral is acceptable for a semantic-only\n   793\t   Draft; version 3 declares semantic support only and the other\n   794\t   profiles fail closed at the gate (§2) until their complete tables and\n   795\t   validation rules are published, with their profiles and code\n   796\t   addresses unchanged.\n   797\t8. **Legacy readers.** Direct version-aware lowering of versions 0–2,\n   798\t   preserving address and custody binding; normalization is a separate,\n   799\t   explicit, partial tool (§9) and a normalized checkpoint has a\n   800\t   different address that never silently replaces an admitted legacy\n   801\t   one.\n   802\t9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n   803\t   retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cell <class>]`,\n   804\t   never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n   805\t   topology, which is why §6 is now defined as the output of a\n   806\t   renaming-invariant refinement-and-search procedure.\n   807\t\n   808\t10. **Receiver work bound** (Architect, round 3; adopted as a\n   809\t    requirement, with the limits left to implementation and\n   810\t    composition). Before an implementation brief, the receiver must have\n   811\t    a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cells, traversal depth, refinement\n   812\t    work and search nodes for §6, with elapsed time as an optional\n   813\t    operational limit on top. Exceeding a local budget produces a\n   814\t    structured resource or capability refusal before any attachment, in\n   815\t    the qualified-unsupported family `datom.world.md` already provides;\n   816\t    it never labels a valid state `:noncanonical-cells`, never falls\n   817\t    back to encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check. The\n   818\t    exact limits and their refusal mapping are implementation and\n   819\t    composition decisions and must not alter canonical bytes.\n   820\t\n   821\t**Proposals, not consequences.** The following are design choices this\n   822\tdocument makes; they follow neither uniquely from the owner's three\n   823\trulings nor from the six invariants, and an implementation brief must\n   824\tname them as choices being accepted: a new body version (3) rather than\n   825\tanother carrier identification; the canonical representative of §6 as\n   826\tdefined by its procedure; the wire unit and pack framing of §3; the\n   827\texplicit fork-policy spelling of §5.5; semantic-only initial scope; the\n   828\tbyte-string and metadata restrictions of §4; binding the row digest to\n   829\tthe carrier version rather than to a named algorithm (§3; the owner\n   830\truled 2026-10-10 that rows use the same digest as code addresses, which\n   831\tis Jing's default, rather than a separate SHA-256 pin); and positional\n   832\trows over EAV (§1). Preserving the complete FFI\n   833\tenvelope (§5.3) is *not* on this list: it is the inherited behaviour,\n   834\tand narrowing it would have been the new protocol decision.\n   835\t\n   836\tStill open after this revision: none beyond the other-profile tables\n   837\t(§10.7) and the work-bound limits (§10.10).\n   838\t\n   839\t## 11. What this does not change\n   840\t\n   841\tCode identity, the contract stamp and profile registry, safepoints,\n   842\tpending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\n   843\tthe dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\n   844\tprivate resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\n   845\tcustody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\n   846\toutcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (two lift kinds and two\n   847\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added, §8). The carrier is a new body\n   848\tversion for the same facts. The\n   849\tArchitect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\n   850\tviolates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\n   460\t  concatenates images with offsets; `absolute-pc`/`image-row`,\n   461\t  `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs are A's, so the relative pc\n   462\t  is the canonical pc.\n   463\t- `rd` = `(code/rd v[pc−1])`; `:resume-mode :write-result` ↔ `act`,\n   464\t  `:return-result` (a tail `:call`, `dest nil`) ↔ `tail`.\n   465\t- `W` = `{phys→virt(pc, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`. The kernel's `live`\n   466\t  operand is the physical image of `L(pc) − {rd}` by phase-5 §2.5, so the\n   467\t  key set equals `saved(pc, rd)`; the validator's rule 8 checks it.\n   468\t- `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and the owner chain at `pc`.\n   469\t- `K` = each continuation frame `{site-pc return-pc regs dest frames}` ↦\n   470\t  `{:return A pc=return-pc rd=dest window=(regs through phys→virt at\n   471\t  return-pc) env=(frames through §4.4 at return-pc)}`.\n   472\t- pending: the engine fields, exactly as today's lift maps them\n   473\t  (`lift-pending!`, `handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n   474\t\n   475\t*Dynamic half, lower.* The inverse: `regs = [[phys(v) V] …]` for `v` in\n   476\tthe window, sorted by physical index; `live` = the sorted physical keys\n   477\t(the kernel's validator then recomputes `body-liveness` and must agree,\n   478\t`live-exact-rule`); `frames` from `E` by §4.4; `dest = phys(rd)`;\n   479\t`resume-mode` by state kind; continuation frames likewise; `:image` the\n   480\trow of `A`; `:site-pc = pc−1`.\n   481\t\n   482\t### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n   483\t\n   484\t**DECIDED** by the phase-5 design §3.2 (the stack emitter's walk) and\n   485\tthe frozen §8.3 remark about pushes; **PROPOSED** as a layout.\n   486\t\n   487\t*Static half.* `H = (:image (derive/stack-image v))`. Two tables per\n   488\tsegment:\n   489\t\n   490\t1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in the\n   491\t   same order as `project` (children, then the parent; bodies FIFO) and\n   492\t   emits one instruction per A instruction **plus** a `:push` after each\n   493\t   child of a call, `:stream-put` target or `:ffi-call` operand. So the\n   494\t   sequence of H instructions **with every `:push` removed** is in\n   495\t   one-to-one order correspondence with A's instructions: the k-th\n   496\t   non-push H instruction corresponds to A's pc `k`. `h->a` and `a->h`\n   497\t   are the two directions of that zip. The harness checks the mnemonic\n   498\t   families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4); the design does not rely on\n   499\t   the claim without the check.\n   500\t2. **operand-stack layout.** For an A pc `p` that is a parking\n   501\t   instruction, `stack-layout(p)` is the vector of virtual registers\n   502\t   whose values the activation's operand stack holds, bottom to top,\n   503\t   when control is at `a->h(p)`: walking the parse tree from the body's\n   504\t   root to the node at `p`, for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n   505\t   the path, the pushed-and-unconsumed children of `n` before `c` are:\n   506\t   for a `:call`, the operator and the operands before `c`; for a\n   507\t   `:stream-put`, the target when `c` is the value; for an `:ffi-call`,\n   508\t   the operands before `c`; for `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor`,\n   509\t   `:stream-next`, `:stream-close`, `:resume`, none (their child is\n   510\t   consumed directly). The vector lists those children's `rd`s in that\n   511\t   order.\n   512\t\n   513\t   **Property checked, not assumed** (§5.6 item 5): `(set\n   514\t   (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`. The\n   515\t   argument: the registers live after a parking instruction are exactly\n   516\t   the already-evaluated siblings that an enclosing expression will\n   517\t   still read, which are exactly what has been pushed and not yet\n   518\t   consumed; the two worked cases are `(f (g x) y)` at the inner call\n   519\t   (stack `[f]`, `saved(4, 2) = {1}`, `analysis_test.cljc:131-139`) and\n   520\t   `(f (if c (g) 2) y)` at `(g)` (stack `[f]`; `saved = {1}` since the\n   521\t   `if`'s `rd` is the call's own `rd`).\n   522\t\n   523\t*Dynamic half, lift.* The stack kernel's wait entry (`:image`, `:pc`,\n   524\t`:frames`, `:stack`, `:continuation [{:return-pc :frames :stack-base\n   525\t:store-of} …]`, `:store-of`; v2 amendment §5.2, `handoff.cljc:897-924`)\n   526\treconstructs: `A` the image's; canonical `pc = h->a(pc_H − 1) + 1`; `rd\n   527\t= (code/rd v[pc−1])`; `W` = zip of `stack-layout(pc−1)` with the\n   528\tactivation's operand-stack slice (from the innermost frame's\n   529\t`stack-base` to the top), key `rdᵢ` ↦ value; `E` by §4.4; `K` = each\n   530\tcontinuation frame ↦ `{:return A pc=h->a(return-pc_H − 1)+1 rd=(code/rd\n   531\tv[pc−1]) window=(zip of stack-layout at that call with the slice\n   532\t[stack-base_i, stack-base_{i+1}) of the operand stack) env=§4.4}`; state\n   533\tkind: `act` unless the parking H instruction is a `:call` with `tail?`\n   534\ttrue, then `tail` with `site = h->a(pc_H − 1)`. A `tail` state has no\n   535\toperand slice of its own: the tail call popped its operands and no value\n   536\tof the current body is read again (frozen §2.4), so the stack kernel's\n   537\tstack at a tail-call park is exactly the enclosing frames' slices.\n   538\t\n   539\t*Dynamic half, lower.* Operand stack = concatenation over `K` outermost\n   540\tfirst of each frame's window laid out by `stack-layout` at its call,\n   541\tthen the current activation's window laid out at `pc−1`; `stack-base`s\n   542\tare the running lengths; `pc_H = a->h(pc−1) + 1`; `frames` by §4.4.\n   543\t\n   544\t### 4.4 Positional lexical frames and the named environment\n   545\t\n   546\t**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 (\"Parameters live in E, not in registers …\n   547\tleaves the de Bruijn register image free to map parameters to its own\n   548\t`L` bank\") and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`); phase-5 §1.2 (the\n   549\towner chain). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the lexical environment as\n   550\tpositional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first, one per enclosing\n   551\tclosure activation, plus a task-wide free environment (v2 amendment §2).\n   552\tThe canonical `E` at a pc of body `b` is\n   553\t\n   554\t```\n   555\tE = (merge free-env\n   556\t           (bind-params params(owner-chain[0]) F[0])      ; outermost closure first\n   557\t           …\n   558\t           (bind-params params(owner-chain[last]) F[last]))  ; the current body's own\n   559\t     ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n   560\t```\n   561\t\n   562\twhere `params(c)` is the `:closure` tuple's parameter vector in A\n   563\t(`(nth v c 2)`) for each closure pc on the owner chain of `b`, outermost\n   564\tfirst (phase-5 §1.2's `chain` reversed), and `bind-params` is the\n   565\tengine's (`engine/bind-params`: rightmost duplicate wins, nil-fill,\n   566\textra arguments dropped). This reproduces exactly the `E` the register\n   567\tevaluator builds on closure entry (`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`:\n   568\t`(merge (:env c) (bind-params (:params c) args))`), because `(:env c)`\n   569\tis the creating activation's `E`, which is itself free-env merged with\n   570\tthe outer chain's bindings. The inverse (`E` → `F`) reads `F[i] =\n   571\t(mapv #(get E %) params(chain[i]))`, which loses nothing the kernel\n   572\tneeds: a parameter shadowed by an inner frame is still read from its own\n   573\tframe by depth, and the de Bruijn kernels never read a name.\n   574\t\n   575\t**Checked, not assumed** (§5.6 item 6): the harness compares the `E` a\n   576\tlayout reconstructs with the `E` the register evaluator holds at the\n   577\tsame safepoint of the same program, for every corpus program, including\n   578\t`:duplicate-param` (`(fn [x x] x)`) and `:nested-closure`.\n   579\t\n   580\t*Closure values.* A de Bruijn closure value (positional marker: segment,\n   581\timage-relative entry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the\n   582\tcanonical closure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`,\n   583\tevaluator §1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the entry's owner\n   584\tpc, `env` by the rule above over the closure's captured `F`, `entry` the\n   585\tbody pc (A's pc). The inverse is `module/lift-closure`'s positional\n   586\tmarker with `F` read back from `env` by the chain. The harness item 7\n   587\tchecks both directions on the `\"closure value\"` and `\"higher order\"` B0\n   588\trows.\n   589\t\n   590\t### 4.5 What a layout never does\n   782\t(§4.4) and recovers the free part as `E` minus the chain's bindings.\n   783\t\n   784\t---\n   785\t\n   786\t## 7. Lift and lower\n   787\t\n   788\t### 7.1 Lift\n   789\t\n   790\t**DECIDED** (draft §9, UCF §7.8): refuse unless quiescent\n   791\t(`:yin.k/not-quiescent`); refuse the holds (`:observe`, unminted cursor,\n   792\tpending close, cursorless `:link-request`) as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n   793\t`:reason-mismatch` (`unliftable-hold`, `handoff.cljc:588`); classify kind\n   794\t(`:parked` when `:value` is a parked record held in `:parked`;\n   795\t`:halted`; `:blocked` when the wait set is non-empty; else\n   796\t`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`); run the dependency closure\n   797\t(`completion/complete` over the register-shaped vectors through the\n   798\tphase-5 scanners, `scan/requirements`, `scan/free-names`) and refuse\n   799\t`:yin.k/unsatisfied` unless `:complete`; number cells (draft §6); walk\n   800\twaits (each `act`/`tail` payload → `:act`/`:tail` rows, its engine fields\n   801\t→ the `:pending` row), parked records, the store slice, module stores,\n   802\tthe result, install children; run `frame/defect` over every frame; emit\n   803\trows and the census; the root's id is the body address.\n   804\t\n   805\tFor a de Bruijn source, the walk is the same over the frames\n    55\t(defn bind-params\n    56\t  \"Zips params with args, nil-filling any params beyond args' length.\n    57\t   Extra args beyond params' length are dropped. §7.7.2: an under-arity\n    58\t   call leaves missing parameter names bound to nil, not absent.\"\n    59\t  [params args]\n    60\t  (into {} (map vector params (concat args (repeat nil)))))\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\t;; =============================================================================\n    64\t;; Application refusals (D6)\n    65\t;; =============================================================================\n    66\t;;\n    67\t;; One vocabulary, in the `:reason` shape, for every kernel:\n    68\t;;\n    69\t;;   :not-applicable      the operator is neither a host function, a\n    70\t;;                        closure nor a continuation; carries a coarse\n    71\t;;                        `:kind`, never the value\n94:                              (when builders {:park-entry-fns builders}))]\n112:           base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n254:              :current-continuation\n258:              :park (-> (put-registers machine seg pc W E K)\n488:  (gc-children [_ _x] nil))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
